  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "The sum of the two \\(5\\)-digit numbers \\(\\overline{AMC10}\\) and \\(\\overline{AMC12}\\) is \\(123422.\\) What is \\(A + M + C?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10\\)",
    "B": "\\(11\\)",
    "C": "\\(12\\)",
    "D": "\\(13\\)",
    "E": "\\(14\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The two numbers have identical digits except in the ones place, so \\(\\overline{AMC12}\\) is exactly \\(2\\) greater than \\(\\overline{AMC10}\\). Removing that extra \\(2\\) from their sum leaves two copies of the smaller number. Thus\n\\[\n\\overline{AMC10}=\\frac{123422-2}{2}=61710.\n\\]\nReading the corresponding digits gives \\(A=6\\), \\(M=1\\), and \\(C=7\\). The numbers are consequently \\(61710\\) and \\(61712\\), which have the required sum. Therefore\n\\[\nA+M+C=6+1+7=\\boxed{14},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
